SOLUTIONS={
1:('DIG-01','Compare from the hundreds column. All numbers beginning with 5 beat 655. Among those, the smallest tens digit is 5, leaving 555 and 556. The smaller units digit selects 555.'),
2:('FRAC-01','Quartering one pizza gives four slices. Two pizzas give 2 × 4 = 8 slices.'),
3:('COUNT-01','Join P to Q and R and draw the diagonal QR. Count only dots strictly inside, excluding all three edges. The interior rows contain 5,4,3,2 and 1 dots. Their sum is 15.'),
4:('DIG-01','Three tenths plus four tenths makes seven tenths: 0.3 + 0.4 = 0.7.'),
5:('RATE-01','Five dollars is 500 cents. Six chocolates cost 6 × 80 = 480 cents, but seven cost 560 cents. He can afford 6, with 20 cents left.'),
6:('DIV-05','Opposite chairs are half a circle apart, or five places in a ten-chair circle. Starting at 9 and moving five places gives 10,1,2,3,4. Chair 4 is opposite.'),
7:('FRAC-01','Read each note using the key. The sequence is worth 2 + 1/2 + 1/2 + 4 + 1 beats. The two halves make one, so the total is 8 beats.'),
8:('COUNT-06','Separate cases by the number of 50c coins. With one 50c coin, add one 10c coin: one way. With no 50c coins, use 0,1,2 or 3 twenty-cent coins and fill the rest with tens: four ways. More than one 50c coin is impossible. Total: 5 ways.'),
9:('DATA-03','Read the graduated scale, not the apparent size of the drawing. A holds 1000 mL, B 750 mL, C 500 mL, D 250 mL and E 500 mL. Thus A currently contains the most water, although it is far from full.'),
10:('GEO-04','A mirror line must reflect the entire pattern onto itself. E’s evenly repeated radial petals give the greatest number of reflection axes through its centre. C has eight axes and B has six; the letter-based or slanting designs have fewer. Choose E.'),
11:('GEO-04','Start at the loop, which lies at the outside of the coil. Each revolution has one painted half and one unpainted half. As you follow the rope inward, the revolutions become shorter, so both kinds of segment become progressively shorter. A shows alternating painted and unpainted stretches that shrink toward the far end.'),
12:('GEO-02','The small tangram square occupies one-eighth of the large square. You can see this by splitting it into two small tangram triangles, each one-sixteenth of the whole. Its area is 64 ÷ 8 = 8 cm².'),
13:('FRAC-02','Two hundred biscuits require 200 ÷ 25 = 8 batches. Each uses 2½ packets of chips, so the total is 8 × 2½ = 20 packets.'),
14:('DIV-04','Two odd numbers add to an even number. Adding a third odd number makes the sum odd. Thus D is always true. The other claims contradict the basic parity rules for sums, differences or products.'),
15:('GEO-01','The outer square has side 36 ÷ 4 = 9 cm. Along any outer edge, one long side and one short side of the identical rectangles fit together. Therefore a rectangle’s length plus width is 9, making its perimeter 2 × 9 = 18 cm. The inner perimeter is not needed.'),
16:('COUNT-02','There are 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24 orders of the four distinct buttons. Pair each order with the order obtained by swapping its first two entries. Exactly one order in each pair has the first larger than the second. Half of 24 gives 12 valid combinations.'),
17:('GEO-01','A straight cut adds one new side to each piece. The original six sides contribute at least six sides in total, even if both cut endpoints are existing vertices. Thus the pieces have at least 6 + 2 = 8 sides altogether. A triangle and quadrilateral have only 3 + 4 = 7, so E is impossible. Cuts through side interiors can split original edges and increase the total to 9 or 10.'),
18:('COUNT-06','The total is 98, so equal groups would need 49 each. Five numbers are multiples of 3, while 29 leaves remainder 2. One group must therefore have sum divisible by 3 and the other remainder 2; neither can be 49. The closest possible sums are 48 and 50. They are achieved by 9 + 15 + 24 = 48 and 3 + 18 + 29 = 50. The minimum difference is 2.'),
19:('LOGIC-03','The numbers 1–16 sum to 136, so every row, column and diagonal totals 34. Column 4 gives its missing entry as 8; row 2 then gives its other missing entry as 11. Call row 3’s first entry f and its third entry g. Row 3 gives f + g = 15. Column 1 gives X + f = 25, and the main diagonal gives X + g = 22. Adding the last two and subtracting the first gives 2X = 32, so X = 16.'),
20:('LOGIC-02','Without blue, there could be at most four reds (no neighbours) and at most four greens (one in each opposite pair). Eight counters would therefore require four of each. Four separated reds must alternate, leaving four alternating greens, which puts green opposite green. So zero blue is impossible. One blue works: around the circle use R,G,R,G,G,R,G,B. There are no adjacent reds or opposite greens. The minimum is 1.'),
21:('DIV-05','Each move rotates the five-disc stack by two places. After five moves the order returns to the start, because ten places is two full circuits. Twenty-one moves have the same effect as one move. Moving the original top red-and-blue pair to the bottom leaves blue at the bottom.'),
22:('ALG-01','Let the lion weigh L kg. The leopard weighs L − 90 and the tiger L − 50. Their sum is 3L − 140 = 310, so 3L = 450 and L = 150 kg. Check: 150 + 60 + 100 = 310.'),
23:('LOGIC-02','Each event awards 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 points. The total 8 + 11 + 5 = 24 means four events. Betty’s 11 requires three wins and one second; Cathy’s 5 requires three thirds and one second, so she never won. Adrienne therefore won the remaining event. Her other three results must total 5: one third and two seconds. She came second twice.'),
24:('ALG-01','Let Jane have a five-cent coins, b ten-cent coins and c fifty-cent coins. Tom has a tens, b twenties and c fives. Equal values give 5a + 10b + 50c = 10a + 20b + 5c, so a + 2b = 9c. At least one fifty-cent coin is needed. For c = 1, the fewest coins occur at b = 4 and a = 1: six coins each. Check: Jane has 5 + 40 + 50 = 95c, and Tom has 10 + 80 + 5 = 95c. With at most five coins, even c = 1 leaves at most four others, whose maximum contribution to a + 2b is 8, too small; larger c is worse.'),
25:('FRAC-04','Let x litres come from A, leaving 18 − x from B. Pure cordial is 0.30x + 0.60(18 − x), and the target is 0.50 × 18 = 9 litres. Thus 10.8 − 0.3x = 9, giving x = 6. Check: 6 litres at 30% plus 12 litres at 60% contains 1.8 + 7.2 = 9 litres of cordial.')}
